HealthCareAI is an AI-based healthcare analytics and decision-support system. A supervised machine learning model (a Random Forest classifier inside a Scikit-learn pipeline) reads the details of a hospital admission – age, gender, blood type, insurance provider, admission type, medication, test result, billing amount, length of stay and admission month – and predicts which of six medical conditions (Arthritis, Asthma, Cancer, Diabetes, Hypertension or Obesity) the admission relates to. Around the model, the system cleans the raw hospital data, produces exploratory tables and charts, compares the model with simple baselines, evaluates it on patients it has never seen, and presents the results in tables, graphs and an HTML report.
The solution is delivered as three command-line steps (train.py, test.py and predict.py) and a Streamlit web interface with a dashboard, a prediction form and a model-results page. By demonstrating how artificial intelligence can support the healthcare industry, it aligns with the theme of “AI Solutions for Industries” and with the Fourth Industrial Revolution’s (4IR) goal of using AI to solve practical business problems through data cleaning, analytics and decision support.
Key finding. On a hold-out test set of 9,908 unseen admissions (patients who never appear in the training data) the model reaches an accuracy of 16.63%. This equals the chance level for six classes (16.67%) and is level with the baseline models (16.30%–16.45%). The data therefore does not contain enough information to predict the medical condition. Reporting this honestly – and preventing the data leakage that would otherwise show a misleading 27% – is an important professional outcome of the project.

